{"summary":"Verified a current, source-backed faculty-fit shortlist for Priya’s single-cell host–pathogen interests and separately checked EMBL’s official recruiting-group list. Scientific fit is supported for three current EMBL group leaders; current student acceptance is verified only at the programme-list level, and none of the three appears on the current recruiting list.","sections":[{"title":"Shortlist","body":"1. Wolfgang Huber — strongest computational/single-cell fit. EMBL identifies him as Group Leader and Senior Scientist in Heidelberg. His group develops statistical and computational methods for single-cell and spatial omics, CRISPR/drug perturbation assays, quantitative imaging, and biomedical/clinical datasets. This directly matches Priya’s scRNA-seq, R/Python, Bayesian-modelling, and malaria-related profile. citeturn0search4turn0search9

2. Nassos Typas — strongest bacterial host–pathogen/microbial-systems fit. EMBL identifies him as Head of Molecular Systems Biology and Group Leader. His group studies bacterial cellular networks, sequencing-enabled genome-wide readouts, antibiotic resistance, and pathogenic or commensal bacteria in microbiomes. An official EMBL project description hosted by Typas specifically involves pathogenic/commensal E. coli, sequencing pipelines, metagenomics, Python/R, and machine learning. citeturn0search1turn0search10turn0search12

3. Jordi van Gestel — credible adjacent fit. EMBL identifies him as a Heidelberg Group Leader. His group combines quantitative single-cell microscopy, functional genomics, high-content CRISPR libraries, experimental evolution, and mathematical modelling to study microbial predator–prey and emerging host–symbiont/host–pathogen interactions. The match is less direct for human infection or malaria than Huber or Typas. citeturn0search2turn0search5"},{"title":"Fit versus acceptance evidence","body":"The official EMBL International PhD Programme recruiting page says its listed groups are actively looking to hire PhD students and that the list is preliminary and may change. The current list includes the Typas Group but does not include the Huber Group or van Gestel Group. Therefore, current EIPP recruitment is verified for Typas at the programme-list level only; it is not verified for Huber or van Gestel. This does not establish that Typas has a project matching Priya’s exact interests, only that the group is currently listed as recruiting. citeturn0search0"},{"title":"Programme timing","body":"EMBL’s official application page states that 2026 Summer Recruitment is closed and that the next 2027 Winter Recruitment opens in September 2026. The page also explains that applicants select groups of interest and that both recruiting and non-recruiting Group Leaders participate in the virtual panel assessment. citeturn0search7turn0search11"},{"title":"Recommended ordering","body":"Prioritise Huber for computational single-cell and spatial-omics alignment; Typas for bacterial infection, microbiome, pathogen genomics, and current EIPP recruitment; and van Gestel as a broader microbial-interaction option. Do not state that Huber or van Gestel are accepting students. For Typas, state only that the group appears on EMBL’s current recruiting-group list, not that a specific project or supervisor slot is guaranteed."}],"drafts":[],"follow_ups":["Recheck EMBL’s recruiting-group page when the 2027 Winter Recruitment opens in September 2026.","If contacting groups later, ask whether a project involving single-cell host-response analysis or pathogen genomics is available within the relevant recruitment round."],"sources":[{"title":"EMBL International PhD Programme — Recruiting Group Leaders","url":"https://www.embl.org/about/info/embl-international-phd-programme/recruiting-group-leaders/"},{"title":"EMBL International PhD Programme — Application","url":"https://www.embl.org/about/info/embl-international-phd-programme/application/"},{"title":"EMBL Huber Group — Quantitative Biology and Statistics","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/huber/"},{"title":"EMBL Typas Group — Systems microbiology","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/typas/"},{"title":"EMBL van Gestel Group — Evolution of microbial life cycles","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/van-gestel/"},{"title":"EMBL project description hosted by Nassos Typas","url":"https://www.embl.org/about/info/scientific-visitor-programme/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/EUR_Project_descriptions_Call-2025.pdf"}],"prepared_result":true,"external_change_confirmed":false,"payment_boundary_reached":false,"purchase_confirmed":false}

{"summary":"Verified an updated shortlist of three current EMBL group leaders, with separate evidence for research fit and PhD-recruitment status. Corrected the prior Bork entry: Peer Bork died on January 16, 2026 and must not be treated as a current group leader.","sections":[{"title":"Updated shortlist","body":"1. Nassos Typas — strongest host–pathogen and microbial-systems fit. The official Typas Group page describes bacterial cellular networks, pathogens, antibiotic resistance, microbiome interactions, and host–pathogen work including Salmonella infection. Typas is also listed on EMBL’s current Recruiting Group Leaders page, which identifies groups actively looking to hire PhD students. citeturn0search7turn0search6

2. Oliver Stegle — strongest single-cell/computational fit. The official Stegle page describes statistical and machine-learning methods for molecular variation, spatial and multi-omics data, and single-cell readouts. His Join Us page explicitly directs PhD applicants to the EMBL International PhD Programme and says the group is always looking for new talent. Stegle is listed among current recruiting group leaders. citeturn0search0turn0search4turn0search6

3. Jordi van Gestel — best additional microbial-interaction fit. His group combines quantitative single-cell microscopy, functional genomics, CRISPR libraries, experimental evolution, and modelling to study predator–prey microbial interactions, with explicit interest in the emergence of host–pathogen and host–symbiont interactions. His official Open Positions page welcomes PhD applicants, although the current EMBL central recruiting list does not list him; therefore, direct group-level openness is evidenced, but participation in the current central PhD recruitment round is not verified. citeturn1search1turn1search5turn0search6"},{"title":"Important correction","body":"The previous answer named Peer Bork as a current third option. That is no longer valid: EMBL announced that Peer Bork died on January 16, 2026. His historic research was relevant, but he should be removed from any current-faculty or current-student-recruitment shortlist. citeturn0search11"},{"title":"Recruitment interpretation","body":"Research fit and student acceptance are separate claims. For Typas and Stegle, there is current official evidence both of scientific fit and listing on EMBL’s recruiting-group page. For van Gestel, the group’s own Open Positions page welcomes PhD-level applicants, but the central EMBL recruiting list is preliminary and does not currently list him; this should be reported as group-level openness rather than confirmed participation in the current round. EMBL states that the recruiting-group list may change before interviews. citeturn0search6turn1search5"},{"title":"Programme timing","body":"As of August 8, 2026, EMBL states that 2026 PhD Summer Recruitment is closed and that the next application round opens in September 2026. citeturn0search10"}],"drafts":[],"follow_ups":["Prioritise Typas and Stegle for the next EMBL application round.","Treat van Gestel as a promising third contact but verify his participation in the September 2026 round before relying on it.","Do not include Peer Bork in the current-faculty shortlist."],"sources":[{"title":"EMBL Typas Group","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/typas/"},{"title":"EMBL Stegle Group","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/stegle/"},{"title":"Stegle Group — Join Us","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/stegle/fellow-opportunities/"},{"title":"EMBL van Gestel Group","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/van-gestel/"},{"title":"van Gestel Group — Open Positions","url":"https://www.embl.org/groups/van-gestel/open-positions/"},{"title":"EMBL International PhD Programme — Recruiting Group Leaders","url":"https://www.embl.org/about/info/embl-international-phd-programme/recruiting-group-leaders/"},{"title":"EMBL announcement on Peer Bork","url":"https://www.embl.org/news/embl-announcements/in-remembrance-of-peer-bork/"},{"title":"EMBL International PhD Programme","url":"https://www.embl.org/about/info/embl-international-phd-programme/"}],"prepared_result":true,"external_change_confirmed":false,"payment_boundary_reached":false,"purchase_confirmed":false}

{"summary":"Official-source review completed: Amara meets the subject-background requirement, but the documented academic qualification does not satisfy UCL Physics and Astronomy’s normal current entry requirement for this research degree. A special-circumstances exception is possible but cannot be verified from the materials.","sections":[{"title":"Current official requirement","body":"UCL’s current Physics and Astronomy MPhil/PhD prospectus states that applicants normally need an upper-second-class UK integrated Master’s (MSci/MPhys) in a relevant discipline, an undergraduate degree followed by a relevant MSc, or an overseas equivalent. It also says an upper-second/first-class Bachelor’s or equivalent may be considered only in special circumstances. UCL further states that the course will not accept applicants qualified only to Bachelor’s level and generally seeks the equivalent of a good UK Master’s by enrolment. citeturn0view0"},{"title":"Applicant-to-requirement reconciliation","body":"Subject prerequisite: SATISFIED on the supplied record. Amara has a BSc in Physics, advanced physics coursework including quantum mechanics, electrodynamics, statistical mechanics, nuclear and particle physics, and computational physics, plus detector-reconstruction research at CERN OpenLab. This is directly relevant to the programme’s Physics/High Energy Particle Physics pathway, which UCL lists among its research groups. citeturn0view0turn1view2"},{"title":"Academic-level prerequisite","body":"NOT SATISFIED under the normal stated route, based on the supplied materials. The record shows a BSc Physics completed and conferred in 2024, with no MSc, MSci, MPhys, or other postgraduate qualification. The CGPA of 4.62/5.00 is strong, but the materials do not establish that it is an overseas equivalent of the required UK Master’s-level qualification. UCL’s Bachelor’s-only exception is discretionary (‘may be considered in special circumstances’), so eligibility through that exception cannot be verified without UCL’s assessment or additional official equivalency evidence. citeturn0view0"},{"title":"Other consequential checks","body":"English-language requirement: unresolved. The current prospectus specifies UCL English Language Level 1, but the materials contain no accepted test result, exemption evidence, or confirmation that the applicant satisfies that level. citeturn0view0

Supervisor/admissions contact: required as an application-preparation expectation, not a stated academic qualification. The current prospectus says applicants should in most cases identify and contact potential supervisors; the department page specifically instructs applicants to contact at least one relevant admissions tutor and to approach an appropriate academic in advance. No such contact is evidenced in the materials. citeturn0view0turn1view2

References: two are required. Professor Ifeanyi Eze is the strongest confirmed academic referee because he taught Amara and supervised her final-year project. Dr Sofia Marin is a plausible professional/research referee. Mr Kunle Aina is weaker because his eight-week internship supervision did not include academic work. UCL requires at least one academic reference from someone who taught the applicant at degree level when two references are required. citeturn0view0turn1view1"},{"title":"Stale or conflicting programme information","body":"The department page contains older entry-cycle information referring to 2025–2026 start dates and older funding wording. The current prospectus is dated for the 2026/27 fee and 2026–2027 entry cycle, lists rolling applications with submission at least three months before the preferred start date, and should control for the current prerequisite assessment. The department page remains useful for research-group and contact expectations, but its old cycle-specific dates should not be treated as current. citeturn0view0turn1view2"},{"title":"What cannot be verified","body":"I cannot verify that Amara qualifies for the Bachelor’s-level special-circumstances exception; that her Nigerian BSc is formally equivalent to UCL’s required standard; that she satisfies English Level 1; that a supervisor has agreed to consider the project; or that full tuition plus living-stipend funding is available for her specific application. UCL lists funding routes and limited scholarships, but the supplied sources do not establish an award for this applicant. citeturn0view0turn1view2"},{"title":"Bottom line","body":"Amara is a strong subject fit for particle-physics research, but on the evidence provided she is not currently verified as meeting UCL’s normal academic prerequisite because she has only a Bachelor’s degree. The application would depend on UCL accepting her under the discretionary special-circumstances route or on obtaining a relevant postgraduate qualification. The next decisive evidence would be written confirmation from UCL Physics/Graduate Admissions about whether her BSc and profile can be considered under that exception, plus accepted English-language evidence if required."}],"drafts":[],"follow_ups":["Obtain written UCL confirmation on the Bachelor’s-level special-circumstances route and overseas qualification equivalence.","Provide an accepted English-language result or documented exemption.","If proceeding, identify a relevant HEP supervisor/admissions tutor and preserve the correspondence."],"sources":[{"title":"UCL Physics and Astronomy MPhil/PhD current prospectus","url":"https://www.ucl.ac.uk/prospective-students/graduate/research-degrees/physics-and-astronomy-mphil-phd"},{"title":"UCL Physics and Astronomy PhD department guidance","url":"https://www.ucl.ac.uk/mathematical-physical-sciences/physics-astronomy/study/phd"},{"title":"UCL Selecting your references","url":"https://www.ucl.ac.uk/study/prospective-students/graduate/how-apply/selecting-your-references"}],"prepared_result":true,"external_change_confirmed":false,"payment_boundary_reached":false,"purchase_confirmed":false}
